{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>When you go out to war against your enemies, etc.</b> 'Your enemies' — in the plural; yet afterward it says 'and He delivers him' — it should have said 'and He delivers them.' And 'against your enemies' — it should have said 'before your enemies.' And in the Yalkut it concludes in the name of the Sifrei, and this is its language: 'Against your enemies' — before your enemies; 'and HaShem your Elohim delivers him into your hand' — if you do all that is stated in the matter, in the end HaShem your Elohim delivers him into your hand. And I have not understood his words: what is 'all that is stated in the matter'? If it is what it says, 'and she shall shave her head,' etc. — behold, he is already delivered into his hand before he does what is stated in the matter, for how could he take captive from him if he is not yet delivered into his hand? And even should you say that there is no proof from the act of captivity, for sometimes captives are taken in war even when the enemy is not yet delivered into one's hand — and proof from 'and they took captive from him' stated in parashat Chukat (Numbers 21:1) — nevertheless it is difficult: whence does he know to interpret 'and He delivers him' as meaning 'in the end He will deliver him into your hand'? Perhaps its meaning is that He will deliver him immediately into your hand." "And it appears that the difficulty for the author of this midrash is: 'and He delivers him' — it should have said 'and He delivers them'; rather, it is certainly speaking of one particular enemy who was included among the many 'enemies' already mentioned. And who is this and which is it? It is none other than the inner foe and enemy — and that is Satan, that is, the evil inclination, who accuses all the more at the time of danger in war, as is explained in parashat Shofetim (Deuteronomy 20) on the verse 'When you go out to war.' And of him it says 'and HaShem your Elohim delivers him into your hand.' If so, it is difficult: how can He promise him that the Holy One, blessed be He, will deliver him into his hand? Behold, after this the matter of the beautiful captive woman is stated, concerning which the Sages said that the Torah spoke only against the evil inclination, etc. — and behold, we see that his impurity is still upon him, for he did not have the strength to stand against his inclination until it caused him to stumble with this beautiful woman. So where is the promise of 'and HaShem delivers him into your hand'? Therefore he explained 'and He delivers him' — in the end He will deliver him into your hand: for if you do all that is stated in the matter, from shaving her head and making her nails — what is written after it? — 'and it shall be if you do not desire her.' Rashi explained: the verse informs you that in the end you will come to hate her, and then assuredly his inclination is delivered into his hand when he hates her. And the essence of this strategy is along the lines of what is said (Ecclesiastes 7:2): 'It is better to go to the house of mourning,' etc., for through this one remembers the day of death, and then assuredly the evil inclination departs. And when she weeps and mourns for her father and her mother, behold, he sits in a house of mourning continually, and through this his inclination recalls the day of death, and then assuredly it departs, and then its desire subsides, and through this he comes to hate her. And since it informs you that in the end you will come to hate her, therefore it says 'in the end' — that HaShem your Elohim delivers him into your hand." "And for this reason it was necessary to interpret 'against your enemies' as 'before your enemies,' for the expression 'upon/against' (עַל) is only fitting for an ordinary war that comes from the heavens above, for in an ordinary war from the heavens the stars fought from their courses (Judges 5:20). Indeed, since it says 'and He delivers him' — in the singular — you are perforce required to interpret it of the evil inclination. If so, when it says 'When you go out to war against your enemies' — in the plural — it certainly included two kinds of enemies: both the external enemy and the inner enemy who accuses at the time of danger. And there is proof for this, for in parashat Shofetim it is written 'When you go out to war against your enemy' (עַל אֹיֶבְךָ) without a yod after the bet, and this speaks of an ordinary enemy alone; but here it says 'your enemies' (אֹיְבֶיךָ) with a yod, indicating many enemies — it certainly included the external and the inner together. And it says 'and He delivers him' — one of them He delivers first, and this is certainly the evil inclination: for if his inclination is not delivered into his hand first, he cannot defeat this external enemy as long as he is sullied with sin. Therefore it was necessary to interpret 'against your enemies' as 'before your enemies,' for the war against the evil inclination does not come from above; rather, the person must be the one who begins, and HaShem completes through him. This is what it says: 'in the end HaShem your Elohim delivers him into your hand' — 'in the end,' that is, after all the deeds that you do, by means of strategies and devices against your inclination, HaShem will send His aid at the end. But in an ordinary war the matter is the reverse: for before you have prepared your heart to go out to war, they have already fought on your behalf from the heavens, and you are but the one who completes, so that it may be called by your name." "<b>When you go out to war, etc.</b> What is 'when you go out'? It should have said 'when you go,' or 'when you fight.' And what is 'to war' (לַמִּלְחָמָה) — it should have said 'to fight' (לִלְחֹם), for 'to war' implies that you are not fighting but have only come to see the war. 'Against your enemies' — it should have said 'before' (כְּנֶגֶד). 'And you take his captive' (וְשָׁבִיתָ שִׁבְיוֹ) — the expression is doubled, and even though our Sages of blessed memory said (Sotah 35b) that this comes to include Canaanite slaves among them, nevertheless it should have said 'and you take captives' (וְשָׁבִיתָ שְׁבִי) — why do I need 'his captive' (שִׁבְיוֹ)?" "And behold, there is room here to interpret this in two ways. The first is along the lines of what the Ramban explained in this parashah on the verse 'When you go out as a camp against your enemies, and you shall guard yourself from every evil thing' (Deuteronomy 23:10) — that is, evil speech, namely lashon ha-ra — so that there be no dissension among them, and they not suffer a blow far greater than that inflicted by the enemies. And this is a clear matter: when there is no peace among those who fight, they are defeated, as happened to the transgressors of Israel in the Second Temple period. Therefore it says 'when you go out' in the language of going out — minimizing the internal war within the city: to say that specifically when you go out of the city you shall spread your hand to war, but within the city there shall be peace among you. And specifically 'to war' you shall go out, and not [go out] from war; and specifically 'against your enemies' you shall go out, and not against those who love you — 'and you shall love your fellow as yourself.' 'And you shall take his captive' — the captive of the hater, and not that you should seek to trap your fellow in your hunt, for all these things cause harm in war. And if you do all these things, then 'HaShem your Elohim delivers him into your hand' — but not without this." "The second path — that he comes to nullify the mindset of your evil inclination, which would persuade you to say: did not this captive come into my hand by my own strength and the might of my hand? And I entered the danger of battle until I prevailed — so why should HaShem impose upon me all these conditions, from shaving to weeping? Therefore he prefaced by saying to him: you speak falsely, for it is not by his strength that he prevails, and only what HaShem has dispossessed before you — that you shall possess. And you are not the warrior; you only go forth from your house to see the battle that has already preceded you — the stars from their courses from the heavens. This is what it says: ״לַמִּלְחָמָה״ — to that battle whose beginning was already; ״עַל אוֹיְבֶיךָ״ — upon Him literally, from the heavens above and not from your opposite, for HaShem has already shot His arrows and scattered them. ״וְשָׁבִיתָ שִׁבְיוֹ״ — the captive of the Holy One, blessed be He, for he was already taken captive by means of the battle. And since HaShem desires to make for you a name of glory throughout all the earth, that the battle be called by your name, therefore ״וּנְתָנוֹ ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ בְּיָדֶךָ״ — for according to what appears to all the nations it is as though your hands did it and overpowered them, but the truth is not so, for the hand of HaShem did this, and for the sake of honor He gave it into your hand."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:26.631149299Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}